# Caldera Scald — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Caldera Scald, the field tower of the Holdfast — a low,
round, bulbous brass pressure drum ringed by a circle of short radial iron nozzle cowls
pointing outward in every direction, like a steam manifold, with no barrel at all — as a 3D
voxel model with a rig a game can pose at runtime. Build it with `voxel-anim` (painting
opaque cells one operation at a time) as a rigged model that reads unmistakably as this
round steam emplacement, holds pressure on its own, and vents a sustained field on demand.
There is no target model to copy.

This test measures creativity and craft, not instruction-following. The brief fixes what
the Scald is and how it must move; it deliberately does not hand you a parts list, joint
placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a tower whose drum pumps and whose cowls
open needs, where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent the rig.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- x runs across the tower, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. y runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to `{{voxel.maxY}}`
  (top). z runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- Up is +y. The Scald is **radially symmetric about the vertical axis**: its nozzle cowls ring
  the drum and point outward in every direction, so it has no single forward face — build it so
  it reads the same from any side.
- The Scald is a **static emplacement founded on the ground**: its base sits flat on the
  ground at `y = 0` and does not move.
- The Scald is deliberately **low and round** — a bulbous, near-spherical drum sitting low
  to the ground, filling the width and depth and rising only a little. Nothing about it should
  read as tall, slim, or squared-off.
- Each part is sculpted separately with `voxel-anim --part <name>`, in this same volume's
  coordinates, positioned where the part sits on the assembled Scald (each cowl already out on
  its side of the ring, the drum already centered).

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Brass — primary plating | `#c8a24a` |
| Brass dark — shadow, underside | `#8a6d2e` |
| Iron — mechanisms, barrels, recesses | `#3a3836` |
| Accent fitting — verdigris (the accent region) | `#2f7d72` |

Do **not** use steel `#b8bcc2`, gold `#ffce54`, or white `#dfeaea` anywhere on the model.
Those are the colors a game paints onto the accent ring at run time to show the tower's
upgrade level (see *The accent region*); baking any of them in makes a base-level tower
look permanently upgraded.

## What the Scald is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the Scald must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A **low, round, bulbous brass pressure drum** founded flat on the ground — a bulging,
  near-spherical body, not a squared-off box. Its shadowed seams and undersides are brass
  dark.
- A **circle of short radial iron nozzle cowls** ringing the drum and pointing outward in
  **every direction**, like a steam manifold. It has **no barrel at all** — no long muzzle,
  no rail, no tube. **No barrel and a ring of nozzles** is the Scald's whole tell: it projects
  a field, it does not shoot, and a reviewer must be able to name it from that alone.
- The **ring of nozzle cowls** is also the accent region below.

The Scald is a **weapon of the Holdfast — brass-and-iron machinery, not a creature**. The
tell is **no barrel at all**: a low, round drum ringed with nozzle cowls, nothing slim or
towering about it and no barrel, tub, or rail of any kind.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions of the drum, how many
cowls ring it and how they are shaped, how they are mounted so they can flex and open, and how
you break the Scald into rig parts. Nothing here prescribes a shape; the test rewards a bold,
characterful design that is unmistakably the Scald and animates convincingly.

## The accent region — a hard contract

A game recolors this model at run time to show the tower's **upgrade level**, by finding every
voxel of one reserved color and repainting it (brass dark at level 0, steel at level 1, gold at
level 2), so one model serves all three levels. That reserved color is the **verdigris accent**
`#2f7d72`, and it is what makes the contract work:

- Sculpt the **ring of nozzle cowls** — and only that — in `#2f7d72`.
- Use `#2f7d72` **nowhere else** on the model. Not a stray voxel, not a highlight, not a seam.
  Every voxel of it is treated as accent fitting.
- The ring must be **one contiguous ring** of cowls and clearly **visible from more than one
  angle** — that is how a player reads a tower's upgrade level at a glance across the
  battlefield.

Everything else — the drum, the recesses — keeps its own color and is never repainted. Get this
wrong and every Scald in the game looks the same level.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author the
motion). Author each with `voxel-anim define-animation` then `add-keyframe`, choosing the period
and each key's `--interp` (`constant`/`linear`/`bezier` or `ease-in`/`ease-out`/`ease-in-out`,
with optional `--in-handle`/`--out-handle`) so the motion reads right — a slow, breathing hold
at idle and a continuous, cycling vent when emitting.

- **`idle`** — the hold (a self-playing idle, `loop = true`, `auto_play = true`). The pressure
  drum swells and contracts slightly as it holds pressure and the nozzle cowls flex, on its own
  with no caller, so the tower reads as live and powered even when it is not emitting. Only the
  drum and cowls move; the ground base stays fixed. It loops seamlessly.
- **`emit`** — the vent (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`; note this animation is named
  **`emit`, not `fire`**). The nozzle cowls open and the drum pumps continuously, venting its
  steam-jet field in a sustained, cycling emission — a **continuous field, never discrete
  shots**. The base stays fixed; only the drum and cowls move.

You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top of this (for example a faint
heat shimmer at rest), but you must not drop or contradict the required `idle` and `emit`
animations, by those names.

## Working the tool

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. Define your own parts with `define-part` (giving each a parent and a pivot), sculpt each
by selecting it with `--part <name>`, set pivots with `set-pivot`, place joints with
`define-joint`, and author the two animations' keyframes with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`.
Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available operations (setting and clearing single voxels,
filling and stroking boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and a mirror plane), the rig subcommands, and the
animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Call
`voxel-anim` once per operation; run `voxel-anim render` and read `parts/<part>.png` (and the
assembled `scene/*.png` previews) between calls to judge each part against this brief — that the
drum reads round and low, the cowls ring it evenly on every side, the ring runs unbroken, and
the animations read with the right breathing weight. Run `voxel-anim render` before you finish so
it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part scores as
empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` renders one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms>
--animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant to check the motion).
